    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A French duchy around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dijon">Dijon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beaune">Beaune</a> and Chalon, ruled by a Capetian line from 1032 to 1361.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#citeaux-abbey">Cîteaux</a>, founded near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dijon">Dijon</a> in 1098, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> the cradle of the Cistercian order and of famous monastic vineyards.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-valois">Valois</a> dukes built a wider Burgundian State; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> fell at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nancy">Nancy</a> in 1477, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> annexed the duchy.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1395 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gamay">Gamay</a> grape uprooted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a>’s vineyards, calling it a ‘very bad and disloyal’ plant, in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pinot-noir">Pinot noir</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

