    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#boso-of-provence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boso-of-provence.jpg" alt="Boso of Provence" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#boso-of-provence">Boso of Provence</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Boson_of_Provence.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> took shape in the late ninth and early tenth centuries in the western part of the old Burgundian lands, the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#autun">Autun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dijon">Dijon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beaune">Beaune</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chalon-sur-saone">Chalon-sur-Saône</a> that remained within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-francia">West Francia</a> when Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-burgundy">Upper Burgundy</a> formed separate kingdoms. Its founder was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#richard-the-justiciar">Richard the Justiciar</a>, count of Autun and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boso-of-provence">Boso of Provence</a>, who gathered several counties under his control, defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Viking</a> army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saint-florentin">Saint-Florentin</a> in 898 and was recognised as the leading prince of the region, with the ducal title attested by around 918. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rudolph-of-france">Rudolph</a> became King of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francia">Francia</a> in 923, and the duchy later passed through several hands until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-i-duke-of-burgundy">Henry the Great</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>, ruled it from 965 to 1002. When Henry died without a legitimate son, his nephew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#robert-ii-of-france">Robert II of France</a> fought for years against Otto-William, Henry’s stepson and adopted heir, to secure the duchy. Robert’s son Henry, on becoming king as Henry I, granted it in 1032 to his younger brother Robert, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#capetian-house-of-burgundy">Capetian House of Burgundy</a>. Robert’s descendants ruled from Dijon until 1361, and the duchy kept its lasting core between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saone">Saône</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#morvan">Morvan</a> and the plateau of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#langres">Langres</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#paris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="Paris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Tour_Eiffel_vue_de_la_Tour_Saint-Jacques,_Paris_ao%C3%BBt_2014_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yann Caradec from Paris, France, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy was a largely rural land of grain fields, forests and vineyards, crossed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saone">Saône</a> and by routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> and Champagne with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lyon">Lyon</a>, the Alpine passes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a>. Its vineyards on the slopes of the Côte d’Or, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dijon">Dijon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beaune">Beaune</a>, were already prized in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and monasteries did much to develop them: the monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#citeaux-abbey">Cîteaux</a> built up the walled vineyard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#clos-de-vougeot">Clos de Vougeot</a> from the twelfth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>’s ordinance of 1395 condemning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gamay">Gamay</a> grape in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pinot-noir">Pinot noir</a> is a famous early attempt to protect quality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> was also a heartland of monastic reform. The great abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cluny-abbey">Cluny</a>, founded in 910 in the neighbouring Mâconnais, held many priories in the duchy, and in 1098 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#robert-of-molesme">Robert of Molesme</a> founded the New Monastery of Cîteaux south of Dijon, the mother house of the Cistercian order. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fontaine-les-dijon">Fontaine-lès-Dijon</a>, entered Cîteaux in about 1113 and made the order one of the most powerful in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. Towns such as Dijon, Beaune, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chalon-sur-saone">Chalon-sur-Saône</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#autun">Autun</a> held markets, and the fairs of Chalon drew merchants trading cloth and wine. Society was dominated by the duke, the Church and a rural nobility, while dependent peasants formed the great majority of the population.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-bouvines"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines.jpg" alt="Battle of Bouvines" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Bouvines_gagnee_par_Philippe_Auguste.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace Vernet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Capetian dukes were great feudal lords who led their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> in the wars of the French kings and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusade">crusade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hugh-iii-duke-of-burgundy">Hugh III</a> commanded the French contingent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> after Philip II returned home in 1191 and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acre">Acre</a> in 1192, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#odo-iii-duke-of-burgundy">Odo III</a> fought for Philip II at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> in 1214, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy">Hugh IV</a> joined the crusade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria">Louis IX</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> in 1248–1250. Military power grew dramatically under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-valois">Valois</a> dukes, who could draw on the wealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#low-countries">Low Countries</a> as well as the duchy. In 1396 the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a> led the Burgundian contingent of the crusade destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nicopolis">Battle of Nicopolis</a>, where he was captured and later ransomed. As duke he crushed the rebellious citizens of Liège at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-othee">Battle of Othée</a> in 1408 and fought the Armagnac faction in the French civil war, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>’s armies took part in the Hundred Years’ War and repeatedly suppressed revolts in Flemish cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> created standing companies of ordinance in 1471 and a powerful artillery train, but his army was routed by the Swiss Confederates at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-grandson">Battle of Grandson</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-morat">Battle of Morat</a> in 1476, and he was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nancy">Battle of Nancy</a> on 5 January 1477.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy.jpg" alt="Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy">Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hugh_IV,_Duke_of_Burgundy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ernest Petit(1835-1918), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the Capetian dukes the duchy was governed much like the royal domain in miniature. The duke ruled with a council of barons and household officers, drew revenues from his domains, tolls and justice, and administered his lands through provosts and, from the thirteenth century, through bailiffs based in districts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dijon">Dijon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#auxois">Auxois</a>, Chalon and the Montagne. The dukes were peers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a> of the king, but they governed their duchy with wide autonomy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hugh-iv-duke-of-burgundy">Hugh IV</a> extended it in 1237 by acquiring the county of Chalon. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#odo-iv-duke-of-burgundy">Odo IV</a>’s marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#joan-iii-countess-of-burgundy">Joan of France</a> brought the Free County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Artois to the ducal house in 1330, uniting the two Burgundies for a time. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-valois">Valois</a> dukes built a far more elaborate administration with Dijon as one of their main centres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> set up a Chambre des comptes at Dijon in 1386 to audit the accounts of the two Burgundies, the ducal Parlement at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beaune">Beaune</a> served as the duchy’s highest court, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#estates-of-burgundy">Estates of Burgundy</a>, representing clergy, nobles and towns, voted taxes. Chancellors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nicolas-rolin">Nicolas Rolin</a>, who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> as chancellor for more than thirty years, directed a government that stretched from Burgundy to Holland, while the ducal palace at Dijon remained the symbolic seat of the dynasty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#well-of-moses"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/well-of-moses.jpg" alt="Well of Moses" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#well-of-moses">Well of Moses</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dijon_-_Puits_de_Mo%C3%AFse_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benjamin Smith, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy’s influence ran through the Church, the arts and dynastic politics. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#citeaux-abbey">Cîteaux</a> and its daughter houses spread the Cistercian order across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, with some five hundred abbeys by 1200, carrying new methods of farming, water management and building. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#henry-count-of-portugal">Henry of Burgundy</a>, a grandson of Duke Robert I, became count of Portugal, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso Henriques</a> became the first King of Portugal, founding the dynasty known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-house-of-burgundy">Portuguese House of Burgundy</a>, which ruled until 1383. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-valois">Valois</a> dukes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> became the base of a power that rivalled the French crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charterhouse-of-champmol">Charterhouse of Champmol</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dijon">Dijon</a> in 1383 as the dynasty’s burial place, where the sculptor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#claus-sluter">Claus Sluter</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#well-of-moses">Well of Moses</a> and worked on the duke’s tomb, works that transformed northern European sculpture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#order-of-the-golden-fleece">Order of the Golden Fleece</a> in 1430, and the court’s ceremony, music and patronage of painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jan-van-eyck">Jan van Eyck</a> set standards of princely display across Europe. His chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nicolas-rolin">Rolin</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hospices-de-beaune">Hospices de Beaune</a> in 1443 and commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rogier-van-der-weyden">Rogier van der Weyden</a>’s altarpiece of the Last Judgement for it. Through the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian of Austria</a>, the Burgundian inheritance and the claim to the duchy passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nancy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-nancy.jpg" alt="Battle of Nancy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nancy">Battle of Nancy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diebold_Schilling,_Battle_of_Nancy,_1477.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diebold Schilling the Elder, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Capetian line ended when the young Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-i-duke-of-burgundy">Philip of Rouvres</a> died of plague in 1361. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-ii-of-france">King John II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> took the duchy as nearest heir and in 1363 granted it to his youngest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a>, as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#appanage">appanage</a>. Philip’s marriage in 1369 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#margaret-iii-countess-of-flanders">Margaret of Male</a>, heiress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a>, Artois and the Free County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a>, made the duchy the core of a much larger Burgundian State, which his successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> extended across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#low-countries">Low Countries</a>. Their ambitions drew them into conflict with the French crown. John the Fearless was murdered on the bridge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#montereau-fault-yonne">Montereau</a> in 1419 by followers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dauphin-of-france">Dauphin</a>, and Philip the Good allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-arras-1435">Treaty of Arras</a> in 1435, when he gained the counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#macon">Mâcon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#auxerre">Auxerre</a>. Charles the Bold sought to link his lands and win a royal crown, but his wars against the Swiss Confederates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lorraine">Lorraine</a> ended with his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nancy">Battle of Nancy</a> in January 1477. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> immediately occupied the duchy, claiming that the appanage had reverted to the crown in the absence of a male heir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> kept most of the Low Countries, but the duchy remained French; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-madrid-1526">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1526, which ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, was never carried out.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> was the principality around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dijon">Dijon</a> that nurtured the Cistercian order and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>’s most celebrated vineyards, and under its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-valois">Valois</a> dukes it became the base of a Burgundian power whose wealth, court culture and rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> shaped late medieval Europe until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>’s death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-nancy">Nancy</a> in 1477.</em></p>

